<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">County of Pallars Jussà</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1011">1011</a>–<a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallars">Pallars</a> Jussà was the lower, southern half of the Pyrenean county of Pallars, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tremp">Tremp</a>, created when Count Sunyer divided his lands in 1011. A frontier county facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lérida</a>, it expanded south by conquest and passed to the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1192.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/county-of-pallars-jussa.jpg" alt="County of Pallars Jussà" width="320" height="517" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Escut_dels_comtes_de_Pallars.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Pallars Jussà, or Lower Pallars, was a county of the Hispanic March in the 11th and 12th centuries, long after the march had ceased to be effectively administered by the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It was a division of the County of Pallars, which had been de facto, and possibly de jure, independent since the late 9th century. Under the testament of Sunyer of Pallars the eldest son, Raymond III, received Pallars Jussà and the younger, William II, Pallars Sobirà. The lower county comprised the valley of Flamicell, the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noguera-ribagorzana">Noguera Ribagorzana</a> and Pobla de Segur, and roughly matched the modern Catalan comarca of Pallars Jussà.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Pallars Jussà was wealthier and more populous than mountainous Pallars Sobirà and was able to expand against the Muslim lands to its south, but Raymond III left the frontier war largely to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnau-mir-de-tost">Arnau Mir de Tost</a>, a baron from the County of Urgell. Under his son Raymond IV comital power was violently reduced by rebellious nobles, backed by Artau I of Pallars Sobirà and Ermengol III of Urgell. The peasants bore arbitrary exactions, forced labour and military service, and the barons claimed customary levies.</p><p>In the war that followed Raymond IV lost most of his fortresses, including Segur, and many nobles renounced their obligations and held their castles as private property. Only with help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a>, received some time before Artau's death in 1081, did he recover his position, and Artau II (1081–1124) is recorded as never having fought his relatives in the lower county. In 1055 Raymond IV had married his daughter Valença to Arnau Mir. The counts ruled until 1124, when the last of the line, Bernard Raymond, who had fought beside Raymond Berengar III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a>, died at the Battle of Corbins.</p><p>His nephew Arnau Mir followed Alfonso the Battler and later Raymond Berengar IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-aragon">Alfonso II of Aragon</a>, who granted him the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraga">Fraga</a>. Around 1161 he founded Vilanova de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallars">Pallars</a> (Palau Noguera), giving it a charter of tax exemption in 1168. His will placed the county under the suzerainty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, but his son Raymond V repudiated it. When the heiress Valença died without heirs, Dolça de Só, daughter of Bernard Raymond, succeeded, and on 27 May 1192 she donated the county to Alfonso and the Crown of Aragon. It was the last major county annexed to the Crown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “County of Pallars Jussà” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1011 Pallars was divided between two brothers into <b>Pallars Jussà</b> and Pallars Sobirà.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-pallars">County of Pallars</a></li></ul></section>
